#: ../../../gui.md:1
msgid "Understanding the GUI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide is an introduction to the Gazebo Graphical User Interface (GUI)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:5
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:7
msgid ""
"Before beginning, you should have [Ionic installed](install). If you "
"installed [from source](install) you will need to [source]"
"(install_ubuntu_src.md#using-the-workspace) the workspace; otherwise, you're "
"good to go!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:12
msgid "Start by getting Gazebo up and running with a sample world:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:14
msgid "gz sim shapes.sdf\n"
msgstr "gz sim shapes.sdf\n"

#: ../../../gui.md:18
msgid ""
"`gz sim` will run the latest version of Gazebo you have installed. You can "
"check if you have more than one version of Gazebo installed by running `gz "
"sim --versions`. For example, if you have Ionic (9.x.x), Harmonic (8.x.x), "
"Garden (7.x.x), and Fortress (6.x.x) installed, `--versions` might return:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:22
msgid ""
"9.0.0\n"
"8.0.0\n"
"7.0.0\n"
"6.0.0\n"
msgstr ""
"9.0.0\n"
"8.0.0\n"
"7.0.0\n"
"6.0.0\n"

#: ../../../gui.md:29
msgid ""
"To ensure you're working with Ionic, include the `--force-version` directive "
"when starting up:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:31
msgid "gz sim --force-version 9 shapes.sdf\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:37
msgid ""
"The version specified to `--force-version` can be just the major version "
"number or the exact number listed in `--versions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:41
msgid "GUI"
msgstr "GUI"

#: ../../../gui.md:43
msgid "After running `shapes.sdf`, you should see the following screen:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:45
msgid "![shapes.sdf](tutorials/gui/shapes.png)"
msgstr "![shapes.sdf](tutorials/gui/shapes.png)"

#: ../../../gui.md:45
msgid "shapes.sdf"
msgstr "shapes.sdf"

#: ../../../gui.md:47
msgid "The interface consists of several sections:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:49
msgid "The Toolbars"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:51
msgid ""
"The toolbar along the top of the GUI contains two buttons, a file menu "
"button (![menu button](tutorials/gui/menu_btn.png)) on the left, and a "
"plugins button (![plugins button](tutorials/gui/plugins_btn.png)) on the "
"right. The file menu button allows you to save the world to a file, save and "
"load interface configurations and customize interface style settings. An "
"interface configuration consists of the plugins you have loaded and the "
"style settings in place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:51
msgid "menu button"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:51
msgid "plugins button"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:55
msgid "![toolbars](tutorials/gui/toolbars.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:55
msgid "toolbars"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:57
msgid ""
"The top left toolbar hanging below the file menu button contains shape "
"buttons (box, sphere, cone, cylinder, capsule) as well as lighting controls "
"(point, directional and spot).  The shape buttons allow you to directly "
"insert a box, sphere, cone, cylinder or capsule model into the world. Simply "
"click on the shape you would like to insert, and place it into the world. "
"The shape will automatically snap onto the ground plane, and its properties "
"will show up on the Entity Tree plugin window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:62
msgid ""
"The following image shows the scene and the Entity Tree after adding three "
"shapes (`box_0`, `sphere_0` and `capsule_0`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:64
msgid "![shape insertion](tutorials/gui/shape_insertion.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:64
msgid "shape insertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:66
msgid ""
"The first five buttons on the second row are for `Transform Control`.  To "
"learn more about this plugin, go to the `Transform Control` section of "
"[Manipulating Models](manipulating_models) tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:69
msgid ""
"The plugins button lists all the available plugins. You can scroll down this "
"list to see all the plugins that are available for your version of Gazebo. "
"When you select one, its interface will appear in the right panel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:73
msgid "The Right Panel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:75
msgid ""
"The GUI contains two plugins that you see in the right panel upon starting. "
"At the top is the Component Inspector, followed by the Entity Tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:78
msgid ""
"Everything in a simulation is considered an \"entity.\" In `shapes.sdf`, the "
"entities listed are the ground plane, each of the shape models, and the sun."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:81
msgid ""
"Entities in the Entity Tree can be expanded to reveal their links, as well "
"as corresponding visuals and collisions, and joints, if they exist."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:83
msgid ""
"You can select entities in the simulation by clicking on their names in the "
"Entity Tree (or by clicking directly on the entity in the scene). You can "
"also hold `Ctrl` and click to select multiple."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:86
msgid ""
"![Selecting multiple entities from the entity tree](tutorials/gui/"
"entity_select.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:86
msgid "Selecting multiple entities from the entity tree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:88
msgid ""
"You can right-click on any plugin to open basic `Settings` or to `Close`. "
"You can (re)open plugins through the plugin menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:91
msgid "The Scene"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:93
msgid ""
"Within the scene is where you'll interact with your simulated world and "
"objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:95
msgid "Currently, you can navigate the scene with a mouse in several ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:97
msgid "**Left-click**: select entity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:98
msgid "**Right-click**: opens menu with options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:99
msgid "*Move to*: center the scene on an entity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:100
msgid ""
"*Follow*: open a submenu with different options to choose the view "
"behaviour, whether it moves or you pan around"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:101
msgid "*Track*: choose an entity for the view to track"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:102
msgid "*Remove*: erase the entity from the simulation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:103
msgid "*Copy*: copy the entity to the virtual clipboard"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:104
msgid ""
"*Paste*: paste the entity from the virtual clipboard creating a duplicate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:105
msgid ""
"*View*: open a submenu with different options to visualize the simulation "
"aspects of the entity like the collisions, the centre of mass, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:106
msgid "**Left-click and drag**: pan around the scene"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:107
msgid "**Right-click and drag**: zoom in and out"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:108
msgid "**Scroll wheel forward/backward**: zoom in and out"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:109
msgid "**Scroll wheel click and drag**: rotate the scene"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:110
msgid "Or use **Shift key + left-click and drag**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:112
msgid ""
"More complex methods for interacting with the entities within a scene will "
"be explained in-depth in the coming tutorials."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:114
msgid "Grid Config"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:116
msgid ""
"The grid visibility in the scene can be adjusted by a plugin. You can adjust "
"features of the world's grid, including cell size, grid position, cell "
"count, and even color."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:119
msgid ""
"Open the Grid Config plugin by selecting it from the plugins button from the "
"toolbar and selecting it from the dropdown list.  The plugin will now show "
"up in the right panel (below the Entity Tree, scroll down if your screen did "
"not display it)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:122
msgid "![Select Grid Config](tutorials/gui/grid_config.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:122
msgid "Select Grid Config"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:124
msgid "World Control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:126
msgid "Along the bottom edge of the scene, you'll notice several buttons:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:128
msgid "![Playback buttons](tutorials/gui/playback.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:128
msgid "Playback buttons"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:130
msgid ""
"From left to right, the options are `Play`, `Steps`, `Reset`,  `RTF` (real "
"time factor)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:132
msgid ""
"Pressing `Play` will start the simulation. You can use the same button to "
"pause the simulation as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:135
msgid ""
"The `Steps` button allows you to step through the simulation at the rate of "
"a step size, or simulation iteration. You can customize step size by "
"hovering over the button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:138
msgid "![hover steps](tutorials/gui/hover_steps.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:138
msgid "hover steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:140
msgid ""
"Pressing `Reset` will clear any unsaved modification to the scene. Clicking "
"it will remove the newly inserted shapes `box_0`, `sphere_0` and `capsule_0`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:142
msgid ""
"At the furthest right end of the scene, you can expand `RTF` to see not only "
"the percentage value that compares real time with sim time, but also those "
"individual values themselves, as well as iteration count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:144
msgid "![RTF expanded](tutorials/gui/rtf_expanded.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:144
msgid "RTF expanded"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:146
msgid ""
"The function of the World Control options will become clearer once you begin "
"manipulating entities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:148
msgid "Next Up"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gui.md:150
msgid ""
"Now that you're comfortable with Gazebo GUI navigation and terminology, "
"let's start learning about more meaningful model interactions with the "
"[Manipulating Models](manipulating_models) tutorial."
msgstr ""
